[33mcommit 3cd1fd51ece83a3b88f7a2da6d2bf4693d6417f1[m
Author: Oddur Sigurdsson <oddurs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 23:56:54 2026 -0400

    gallery: judge visual changes by pictures, the way bench judges by numbers (#27)
    
    * gallery: judge visual changes by pictures, the way bench judges by numbers
    
    X0 of docs/roadmap/experience.md, and row 1 of the priorities order.
    `zig build gallery` renders ten canonical screens headless through the
    real parser, grid and renderer and diffs each against a committed PNG,
    reporting differing pixels and the worst single-channel delta.
    
    The sprint called the risk low -- "the flag exists; this is scripting
    around it" -- and that was wrong in three places.
    
    Rendering needed no display: SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy gives SDL a backend
    with none, and RenderReadPixels still returns a correct frame. (The
    `offscreen` driver, which sounds right, cannot create a window.)
    
    A 2x capture was impossible on a 1x machine, because the renderer asked
    the display how dense it was. --scale tells it to pretend, and init now
    sizes the window so its pixel size fits the grid at that scale; without
    that, 2x drew double-size glyphs into a single-size window and showed
    half the columns.
    
    Captures needed to be PNG: 152 KB of committed images would have been
    about a megabyte as BMP. src/png.zig is ~100 lines over std.compress
    .flate -- 8-bit RGBA, filter 0, encode and decode. We link libc and the
    system's frameworks and nothing else, and a picture format is not a good
    reason to break that.
    
    Building it found two real bugs. The app could lose the tail of a
    program's output, because the loop ends when pty.exited() notices the
    child is gone while its bytes are still buffered; the exit path now
    joins the reader and drains. And --screenshot fired one second in, so it
    could not capture a program that prints and exits -- the final frame is
    now captured on the way out, which also makes it deterministic.
    
    Done when, from the sprint: a one-pixel change in the cursor shows up as
    a diff. Drawing it one pixel narrower moves 17 pixels at 1x and 34 at
    2x; changing its colour by one in a single channel moves 153 at a worst
    delta of exactly 1.
    
    Scenes are scripts printing fixed bytes, not vim and htop as the sprint
    suggested: their output varies with version, locale and the files lying
    around, and a pixel diff cannot tell that apart from a regression. Real
    program screens want recorded streams, which A0 produces.
    
    Non-gating in CI, like the bench, and it uploads the renders.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * gallery: fix five defects code review found, including a hang I added
    
    The drain added for the tail of a program's output waited for the pty to
    go quiet, which a live child never allows: closing the window while a
    command was producing output hung the app until it was killed. It now
    runs only when the child has actually exited, and under a deadline.
    
    The gallery was not headless. std.process.spawn with no environment map
    hands the child an empty environment, so the SDL_VIDEODRIVER the build
    step set never reached the app -- every reference was rendered through
    the real Cocoa and Metal backend while the docs, the build comment and
    the CI job all said otherwise. Run genuinely headless, all ten failed;
    five on size alone, which skips the pixel diff, so the arbiter would
    have been blind for half its captures. gallery.zig now builds the
    child's environment itself. References regenerated.
    
    A capture depended on the machine that took it: window size is set in
    logical units, so want_px / density rounds, and on a 2x display an odd
    pixel height became even. Captures are cropped to the grid's own pixel
    size, which is the reproducibility --scale exists for.
    
    png.decode multiplied header fields in u32 before bounding them, so a
    70-byte file claiming 0x40000000 x 1 panicked in a safe build and
    returned a bogus image in a fast one. Dimensions are bounded first, and
    the arithmetic is usize.
    
    One unreadable reference aborted the whole run instead of reporting that
    capture and carrying on with the other nine.
    
    Tests 142 -> 143. Verified the gallery now renders identically whether
    or not SDL_VIDEODRIVER is already set outside it, and that the cursor
    mutation is still caught.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * docs: correct the gallery example size after the crop fix
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * docs: record what the gallery's first CI run measured
    
    Every capture's size matched and the one scene made only of rectangles
    was byte-identical, while all nine containing text differed by 1-3% of
    pixels. That isolates the whole cross-machine difference to glyph
    rasterization, which turns the case for D1 into a measurement with a
    testable prediction: those nine should go to zero when it lands.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

