[33mcommit f8e1d9441190c98457e5b1499d55ceab9db7da56[m
Author: Oddur Sigurdsson <oddurs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 23:29:35 2026 -0400

    ci: record the release checksum by basename (#26)
    
    The published .sha256 named `dist/terminator-...tar.gz`, the path it had
    on the runner. Anyone downloading both files puts them side by side, so
    `shasum -a 256 -c` looks for a `dist/` directory that is not there:
    
      dist/terminator-v0.1.0-aarch64-macos.tar.gz: FAILED open or read
    
    Found by downloading the v0.1.0 release and checking it the way a user
    would. Recording the basename makes the file verify where it lands.
    
    Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

[1mdiff --git a/.github/workflows/release.yml b/.github/workflows/release.yml[m
[1mindex b64e537..80e0546 100644[m
[1m--- a/.github/workflows/release.yml[m
[1m+++ b/.github/workflows/release.yml[m
[36m@@ -70,7 +70,10 @@[m [mjobs:[m
           cp zig-out/bin/terminator "dist/${name}/"[m
           cp README.md LICENSE "dist/${name}/"[m
           tar -czf "dist/${name}.tar.gz" -C dist "${name}"[m
[31m-          shasum -a 256 "dist/${name}.tar.gz" > "dist/${name}.tar.gz.sha256"[m
[32m+[m[32m          # Record the basename, not the build path: whoever downloads[m
[32m+[m[32m          # these two files puts them side by side, and a checksum naming[m
[32m+[m[32m          # `dist/...` makes `shasum -a 256 -c` fail to find its own file.[m
[32m+[m[32m          ( cd dist && shasum -a 256 "${name}.tar.gz" > "${name}.tar.gz.sha256" )[m
 [m
       - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4[m
         with:[m

[33mcommit e464c929519db24bcb642d03ce550cad53a9dee8[m
Author: Oddur Sigurdsson <oddurs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 23:22:51 2026 -0400

    ci: fix the release build, which had never run (#25)
    
    Pushing v0.1.0 ran release.yml for the first time and it failed:
    
      src/pty.zig:13:11: error: C import failed
      cimport.h:1:10: error: 'util.h' not found
    
    The build step passed -Dtarget=aarch64-macos. Naming the target
    explicitly stops Zig using the macOS SDK's headers, so @cImport of
    <util.h> for forkpty cannot resolve. CI's own test job builds without
    -Dtarget and has always passed, which is why this went unnoticed -- the
    release path had no tag to run on until now. A cross-compile is
    impossible here anyway, as the matrix comment already says: SDL3 and
    FreeType come from Homebrew through pkg-config.
    
    Also checks that the built binary reports the tag it was built for.
    V0 moved the version to build.zig.zon so nothing has to be kept in step
    by hand; this asserts it about the artifact people actually download,
    which the changelog and zon gates do not cover.
    
    Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

