[1mindex 43b0e27..b64e537 100644[m
[1m--- a/.github/workflows/release.yml[m
[1m+++ b/.github/workflows/release.yml[m
[36m@@ -28,7 +28,6 @@[m [mjobs:[m
         include:[m
           - os: macos-15[m
             arch: aarch64[m
[31m-            target: aarch64-macos[m
     steps:[m
       - uses: actions/checkout@v5[m
         with:[m
[36m@@ -43,8 +42,25 @@[m [mjobs:[m
         with:[m
           version: ${{ env.ZIG_VERSION }}[m
 [m
[32m+[m[32m      # Deliberately no -Dtarget. The runner is already the target, and[m
[32m+[m[32m      # naming it explicitly stops Zig using the macOS SDK's headers, so[m
[32m+[m[32m      # `@cImport` of <util.h> for forkpty fails with "'util.h' not found".[m
[32m+[m[32m      # A cross-compile is impossible here anyway -- see the matrix comment.[m
       - name: Build[m
[31m-        run: zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast -Dtarget=${{ matrix.target }}[m
[32m+[m[32m        run: zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m      # The artifact must report the tag it was built for. V0 moved the[m
[32m+[m[32m      # version to build.zig.zon so that nothing has to be kept in step by[m
[32m+[m[32m      # hand; this is the check that says so about the thing users download.[m
[32m+[m[32m      - name: Check the binary reports the tag[m
[32m+[m[32m        run: |[m
[32m+[m[32m          tag="${{ inputs.tag || github.ref_name }}"[m
[32m+[m[32m          got=$(./zig-out/bin/terminator --version)[m
[32m+[m[32m          echo "$got"[m
[32m+[m[32m          case "$got" in[m
[32m+[m[32m            "terminator ${tag#v}"|"terminator ${tag#v} ("*) ;;[m
[32m+[m[32m            *) echo "binary reports '$got', expected version ${tag#v}" >&2; exit 1 ;;[m
[32m+[m[32m          esac[m
 [m
       - name: Package[m
         run: |[m

[33mcommit 02c68ec89ff43284401f3679b7f059ebfc2b22be[m
Author: Oddur Sigurdsson <oddurs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 23:17:24 2026 -0400

    release: make 0.1.0 true (#24)
    
    * release: make 0.1.0 true
    
    V0 of docs/roadmap/releases.md. The repository described a release that
    never happened: CHANGELOG.md had a 0.1.0 section linking to a v0.1.0 tag
    that did not exist, and the version was a string constant in pty.zig
    because that is where TERM_PROGRAM_VERSION is set.
    
    One source of truth. build.zig.zon's .version is read by build.zig and
    handed to the new src/version.zig as a build option, together with the
    short commit from `git rev-parse` (empty outside a checkout, so a build
    from a release tarball still works). pty.zig imports it instead of
    owning a copy; --doctor and the bundle's Info.plist will read the same
    string when P4 and P0 arrive.
    
    --version, and -V, print `terminator 0.1.0 (faac302)`. The commit is
    what turns a bug report into a build someone can check out. parseArgs
    now returns an Action union rather than an optional, so --help and
    --version are reported to the caller instead of this file calling
    process.exit -- which would take the unit tests with it.
    
    The workflow checks the changelog. scripts/changelog-section.sh prints a
    version's section and fails when there is none, so release.yml refuses to
    publish a tag the changelog does not describe, and uses the hand-written
    section as the release body with the generated PR list under an "All
    changes" heading beneath it rather than instead of it. The publish job
    gained the checkout it needs to read either.
    
    The 0.1.0 entry now describes the commit that will be tagged, which
    includes the performance work, rather than the state it was written
    against.
    
    Tests: 128 -> 133. The gate is mutation-tested against a missing section,
    an empty one and one that is only blank lines.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * release: fix seven defects code review found in V0
    
    --version wrote to stderr, so the one machine-readable thing the sprint
    added could not be captured: `v=$(terminator --version)` yielded an empty
    string. --help and --version now go to stdout, where an answer belongs.
    
    The release gate checked the changelog but nothing checked the tag
    against build.zig.zon's .version, so a v0.2.0 tag could ship a binary
    reporting 0.1.0 -- precisely the drift one source of truth exists to
    remove. scripts/zon-version.sh reads the number and release.yml refuses a
    tag that disagrees with it.
    
    gitCommit reported an *enclosing* repository's HEAD: `rev-parse` walks up
    out of the build root, so a source tarball unpacked inside somebody's
    working tree was stamped with their commit -- a provenance string
    pointing at an unrelated commit, worse than the empty one documented. It
    now requires the build root itself to carry a .git.
    
    changelog-section.sh ended a section at a `## ` line inside a fenced code
    block, and exited 0 while doing it, publishing a body cut off mid-fence.
    Fences are tracked now. Its blank-line trim also assumed LF, so a CRLF
    changelog kept the blank lines it claims to strip.
    
    version.zig asserted the short commit is exactly 7 characters; git
    documents --short as a minimum and lengthens it when 7 would be
    ambiguous, so that test would have failed one day for no related reason.
    
    cli.zig claimed --help and --version win over everything, and they did
    not: value-taking flags matched first, so `terminator --shell --version`
    took "--version" as a shell path and opened a window to exec it. A first
    pass over argv makes the claim true.
    
    The gates are shell, so `zig build test` cannot see them; they decide what
    ships, so scripts/test-changelog-section.sh gives them 17 assertions and
    CI a step to run them. Zig tests 133 -> 135. Each fix is mutation-tested.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

